 {:seon.task/name "stale-sections-are-a-part-of-the-page"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner (2026-10-26): :seon.doc/read as an error's subject is a code smell, and so is the machinery behind it; the database knows exactly what changed, so only changed parts render again and the rest hit the render cache; test it with agents in the REPL, then propose the design, and do not build yet another system. What exists (checked 2026-10-27): `seon.problems/stale-sections` compares form ids recorded at load (:seon.doc/read, `seon.doc/read-map`) with current ones and reads history for meaning (`seon.program/meaning`: :var/doc, :var/contract); a section loaded with no read asks git (`seon.root/wiki-baseline`, the :history effect `seon.fx/wiki-history`, `seon.doc/baseline-plan`, `baseline-reads`, `baseline-failed`; :seon.doc/baseline-unread). Measured in a lab root (412 sections, 2,990 vars, 1,155 citations): 0 sections held a :read, because boot and each watcher batch load docs before source (`my.root/load-kinds!`), so the read is taken against a program that is not there yet. Experiments, in a Seon agent's REPL on a lab store branch: (1) the rule 'a section is stale when a cited var has a datom on its meaning in a transaction after the section's text or review' against today's scan after random edits of a docstring, a contract and a body (the results are exactly the sections citing a var whose meaning changed after them; a body edit makes none); (2) its cost in contracted calls and bytes at 412 sections and at x10 (one eavt seek per citation against today's scan); (3) the same as a part of the page on the one path (view, page, diffs) whose input is ctx values, so an unchanged var is identical? and the part's memo a pointer test; (4) source loaded before docs at boot and per batch so a section written with its code is current. If they bear out, it deletes :seon.doc/read, :seon.doc/baseline-unread, wiki-baseline, baseline-plan, baseline-reads, baseline-failed, read-map, the :history effect, the form-id and history comparison in stale-sections and the form ids of :seon.doc/reviewed; given up: drift before the store existed and Reviewed lines in old commit messages. Related: seon.problems/a-section-goes-stale-when-what-it-cites-changes-meaning (the rule, already in stale-sections). Proof to write: the property in (1) and a cost proof in (2)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-value-is-rendered-by-the-newest-render-of-its-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-29: \"automatic per eval result auto rendering and to override by just writing a function that renders that. A render function is just a function that takes in the data we need to render and optional args we can auto inject like the current db or even the current ctx if they define the inputs and returns hiccup (seon.render/html) ... just a query which gets the default and any other render functions and sorts them by newest first\"; \"if we don't know what something is we return a warning/error so the agents are encouraged to fix it and we only auto render things we know what they are and have specified render functions\".

What exists: seon.render/render-of selects a render by the value's own :seon.render/html, then the one its schema declares (:seon.render/html on the schema entry, seon.render/declared), then the printer; seon.render/reads fills :seon.ctx/ctx and :seon.db/db when a render's opts contract names them; the program db holds each var's contract (:var/contract). A schema's declared render is global: an agent re-pointing it changes everyone's view at merge.

Correct:
1. A render is any function whose contract is [:=> [:cat opts K] :seon.render/html] for a named schema K; its opts contract names what it is injected (:seon.ctx/ctx, :seon.db/db).
2. A value's renders are one query over the db's :var/contract: the functions taking K, newest first by the transaction of their contract; the first is used. The schema's declared render is the oldest candidate, the default. The answer is a cache keyed by the db's t (AGENTS 5).
3. What a value is: a ctx key is its schema; an entity its identity attribute's schema; an eval's value the named return schema of its outermost call (after macroexpansion) when that is a program var.
4. Unknown: printed by the one printer, never guessed (no validation against candidates). When the outermost call is a program var whose return schema is generic (:any, :map), the answer carries a warning naming the var and the fix (name its return schema); code that is not the program's (clojure.core, interop, a literal) is printed with no warning, since there is nothing in the program to fix.
5. Each eval whose value has a render is drawn as a tile beside the conversation (render-ctx's p-tiles today).

Replaces: the schema-declared render as the only way to choose one. Scoping: a render the agent defines wins on its branch at once; merged, it is the newest on main."
  :seon.task/kind :design
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-render-tag-is-a-checked-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: a value's render schema is named in metadata (:seon.render/schema) or guessed from its keys (seon.render/schema-of), never validated, and many tag schemas are bare [:map] (seon.render.edn: :seon.agent/state, :seon.context/run, :seon.context/status, :seon.test/proved, :seon.render/render-ctx, root-ctx, root-input, :my.agent/lines, :seon.context/namespaces). Metadata does not survive the store, so the record grew a second channel (:schema; 952fe4f3a0, 21e364cabc, 710b32c164), and new renders hung on with-meta (0a58e8aa31, 51825107d1). The change: one constructor seon.render/as [k v], the only writer of :seon.render/schema, validates v against k while contracts are on; each tag schema gets its real keys; if the record's tag then lives in the value's declared shape, the record's :schema channel goes, and error-key sniffing goes (an error's :seon.error/type is its schema). Sweep the 32 sites of the key in src. Docs: docs/render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-tagged-value-is-checked-against-its-tag}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-walk-of-a-branch-draws-the-branchs-layout"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: root's walk of an agent's branch (seon.render.proc/branch-walk!, and its cold draws) reads seon.render/declared-var under root's registry, not the branch's: seon.render.proc binds none, so a layout the agent registered on its branch draws on its own walk (seon.repl/the-walk, what its model reads) and not on the page root serves for an ended agent. Found by the sweep of step 2 of the finished task one-way-to-make-what-an-agent-is-sent, whose fix for a walk's held layout has no reach here. What should be true: docs/ctx.md's page is the agent's to change holds for every reader of its page, live or ended; seon.repl/branch-registry reads a branch's schema changes, and the same read serves root's walk. Proof: an ended agent's page, drawn by root, shows the layout its branch registered."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/roots-walk-of-a-branch-draws-the-branchs-layout}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-var-call-a-render-cannot-draw-says-why"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: a render's var call whose contract does not ask for :seon.ctx/ctx or :seon.db/db (seon.render/inputs) is kept as a call by seon.render/emit, with no error: an agent's new part printed as `[#'lab.ui/where {} 7751]`. A trap once every agent writes renders. Tried in this form, any var call as a component: one line, but drawing-one-list-costs-that-list-not-the-page goes from 616 to 1,563 calls, each seon.render/html becoming a component, and the kept call the model reads as structure is gone. So not that. What should be true: the rule that tells a component from a kept call is stated by data, and the call an agent meant as a part says why it stayed a call. The lab settles which."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-var-call-a-render-cannot-draw-says-why}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-debug-page-reads-a-branchs-own-renders"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: a branch's own render draws on the chat, through the agent's units, and not on its debug tab: seon.render.proc's debug value runs root's code (seon.context/wire-turn), under root's registry, so a recorded request is re-rendered by main's renders. What should be true: while the agent lives, its latest request is asked of it, as seon.render.proc/ask-parts asks its parts. A past turn's replay stays root's, and says so. Beside it: seon.render/roots-walk-of-a-branch-draws-the-branchs-layout."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-debug-page-reads-a-branchs-own-renders}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-page-draws-at-a-narrow-width"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-06 in the browser pane at 554 wide: the agent's page (seon.render.ui/render-ctx, seon-page, seon-columns in resources/public/css/input.css) screenshots blank and its document overflows the width; at 1400 wide its three columns draw. What should be true: a page at phone width stacks its columns with no horizontal scroll. Proof: a browser check at 375 wide."
