 {:seon.task/name "both-renders-run-in-the-components-tracked-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: seon.render/model runs a part's model render outside the component's run, lazily when a request asks, as (f {} v), kept by the part's revision. So what it reads is invisible to the walk, it cannot ask for the db, and an agent's own choice of render does not redraw. The html render runs inside (seon.render/part). The owner, 2026-11-04, asked why re-rendering has problems if everything is tracked; this is why. What should be true: both renders run inside the component's tracked run, given opts, the model's always since a model always reads and the html lazily if a page cost asks for it. Measure drawing-one-list-costs-that-list-not-the-page before and after; a page-only part with data pays for a render nobody reads, unmeasured. Part of the core."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-model-render-reads-the-db-and-is-tracked}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-layout-change-leaves-no-old-parts"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: after an agent's layout changes, its old parts stay on the page for a pass. Not traced. seon.repl/the-walk disposes the old walk and makes a new one, and root's render proc sends a unit only when its revision moved, so the cause may be units a new walk does not name staying in the tab. Trace it first; the proof follows the cause. What should be true: the page after a layout's change holds the new layout's parts only, on the first draw."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-layout-change-leaves-no-old-parts}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-edit-reaches-disk-when-asked"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-06: edits go into the database, and reach disk when asked; asked again about the commit he said to do what's simplest. Decided, the owner, 2026-11-07: \"The DB is the source of truth we need to merge to main cleanly and then we can efficienltly sync to disk if we want. That shouldn't be automated yet have your agents manually call it and verify it's working right.\" So a merge writes no file (:seon.config.source/write-back-on-merge), and `my.root/write-back!` (seon_write_back) syncs by hand. An Edit fold on wiki sections (the system message too) and on a var's source posts `seon.repl/edit!` to the agent's REPL; a Merge button is the ask. An agent with a task merges with its work. An agent with none gets a task made on its branch, its plan the edit's summary, judged by the normal gate on the tests the change reaches (the done rule refuses a task with no proofs: an edit's kind passes that one reason) and merged by the reconciler. Root's watcher stays: a disk change loads as now; if a changed file also has an edit on main not yet committed, and that shows up in practice, it is not loaded and the page says so. A task's plan is no file `seon.source/changed-files` knows, so an edited plan stays on the db for now. Waits on agents merging at all: Context's real run of 5d5cad3023 merged 0 of 5, and this button rides that path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-spawned-with-a-task-keeps-its-branch-on-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-06, and narrower than first filed: `seon.root/observed` leaves an agent on a task's branch to its start's fact (`seon.task/start-tx`, written by the reconciler and by attach), so a real run is fine. Only a hand-typed `(seon.root/spawn! sys {:task t :branch :b})`, which no start follows, leaves main with no :seon.agent/branch, and its debug tab then says `no model turn yet` after a model turn its branch records. Make a page that can't read an agent's branch say that rather than that nothing happened; then `spawn!` with :task either writes the start or refuses."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/a-spawned-agents-debug-shows-its-model-turn}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-draws-every-session-with-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "Was `page-walks-only-the-rows-shown` (a `Completes:` line of 5d5cad3023 names that, so the entry is renamed to load): removed from reading, and its named proof `seon.render-cost-test/a-page-walks-only-the-rows-shown` was never in test/ (no deftest of that name exists). What its old plan described is gone: `seon.render/walk`, `seon.render.ui/page` and the unit walk were deleted in 7e999e41bf (every page is the render of a ctx through `seon.render/walker`), and an agent's page sends all of its conversation by design (docs/render.md, owner 2026-10-28: no history budget). What is left on root's page: `seon.render.ui/work-list` pulls (`d/pull db session-attrs`) every session with a task, `(d/datoms db :aevt :seon.agent/task)`, then draws a `seon.render.ui/session-card` for each, which pulls the same attributes again; finished ones are folded in a closed `details` but drawn. The test `root-reads-what-is-going-on` in seon.render-test asserts that every session with a task is a card. Its cost is linear in the sessions with a task and no cost proof measures it; not measured here (the test's helper `sessions-db` was unbound when called from a root eval, so no n was run)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-person-removes-a-message-from-their-page"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent removes a message with `swap!` on `ctx`, but a user with no process has no REPL to do it, so `seon.render.ui/user-messages` only grows. Correct: the message's render on a user's page carries a remove control, a call that retracts this user's own `:seon.message/to` on main through root's writer, by the existing `/call` path with no new route. Also: a message's `from` is a per-boot id like a1, so it should link to the agent by its key. Proof: removing one leaves another recipient's, and the page redraws. See docs/users.md#messages."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
